# American English: only the lines that differ from English. The rest is English.

settings-appearance-keywords = color colors color colors accent theme themes look skin glass dark light mode sunshine bright icons font cursor pointer screen reader blind read aloud accessibility labels tooltips names pointing

settings-appearance-help = Choose light or dark, colors, icons, fonts and the pointer, or switch on the screen reader. Some programs only show a change after you reopen them.

settings-appearance-theme-about = A theme is a whole look in one file: colors, corners, sizes and spacing. Press a theme to try it. Every Trindle window changes at once. Unless you press Keep, the look you had comes back by itself after 30 seconds, so you're never stuck with a theme you can't read. If a theme has both a dark and a light look, Dark and Light buttons appear beside its picture. Light is easier to see in bright sunshine, and apps from Apps use it the next time they open. A theme with one look has no such buttons. Your highlight color stays your own in every theme. Trindle only reads a theme you add and never runs it, and it makes any hard-to-read words clearer. To bring back Trindle's own look from a terminal, run trindle-settings --theme default.

settings-appearance-dot = Color you typed

settings-appearance-hex-spoken = Any color, as a hex code such as #fbc711

settings-appearance-any-colour = Any color

settings-appearance-colour-title = Color

settings-appearance-colour-about = The highlight marks buttons, switches, the ring as it fills, and where you are. Trindle's own windows change at once, and the ring changes the next time it opens. If a color is too dark to see on a card, Trindle lightens it until it can be read. In a light look, Trindle darkens a color that is too pale. Choose Dark or Light on a theme's own tile, under Theme.
